  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO- PeaceNexus Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Conduct a participatory gender sensitive conflict analysis. This approach seeks to facilitate a process of collective analysis, based on views and experience of those directly affected by conflict and insecurity. Conducted at community level, it will bring people together to not only identify and discuss key challenges their community faces, but to also grasp which opportunities for peace do already exist within the humanitarian and development initiatives. To make &#x2018;participation&#x2019; meaningful, a wide range of people shall be involved in discussions and focus groups, with all having the opportunity to influence and shape the discussions, thus the outcome of the analysis. A consultant shall be identified to conduct the research.

2. Organize a dissemination workshop for Oxfam staff and stake holders on the findings of the study. This dissemination workshop shall be organized by Oxfam in coordination with the consultant. This activity shall be conducted and an action developed on how to carry the recommendations of the study forward.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="1" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Denmark (IBIS)</narrative>
  </participating-org>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib is one of the 17 national affiliates of the Oxfam International Confederation. It started life in 1956 as the Netherlands Organisation for International Assistance (Novib), and joined forces with Oxfam in 1994.  Working together in confederation, the Oxfam affiliates can speak with one clear and compelling voice on issues of global concern: climate change, health and education forall, agriculture, trade, and conflicts and emergencies.  Oxfam established this country office to support local organizations and projects.  Through this local office Oxfam Novib directly contributes towards this project&#x92;s implementation.</narrative>
